        "title": "Information Geometry",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Information geometry treats the set of probability distributions as a Riemannian manifold, with the Fisher information metric as the natural geometry. This gives a coordinate-free framework unifying statistics, information theory, and optimization."
    },
    {
      "id": "09-INFORMATION-GEOMETRY.md#003",
      "path": [
        "09-INFORMATION-GEOMETRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:probability-statistics:09-information-geometry, git-history:probability-statistics:09-information-geometry]",
        "canonical_path": "probability-statistics/09-INFORMATION-GEOMETRY.md",
        "concepts": "[information, geometry]",
        "current_path": "probability-statistics/09-INFORMATION-GEOMETRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:probability-statistics:information-geometry",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "probability-statistics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[information, geometry]",
        "section": "probability-statistics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Information Geometry",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                   INFORMATION GEOMETRY LANDSCAPE                 |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  STATISTICAL MANIFOLD S                                          |\n|  = {p(x; theta) : theta in Theta}                               |\n|  Each point is a probability distribution.                      |\n|                                                                  |\n|  GEOMETRY ON S:                                                  |\n|  Fisher information metric g_{ij} = E[partial_i log p * partial_j log p]\n|  (Riemannian metric on the manifold of distributions)           |\n|                                                                  |\n|  TWO NATURAL CONNECTIONS:                                        |\n|  e-connection (exponential): flat for exponential families      |\n|  m-connection (mixture): flat for mixture families              |\n|  These are dual connections: together they determine the geometry|\n|                                                                  |\n|  DIVERGENCES (asymmetric distances):                            |\n|  KL divergence D_KL(p||q)                                       |\n|  alpha-divergences (generalization)                             |\n|  Bregman divergences                                            |\n|                                                                  |\n|  APPLICATIONS:                                                   |\n|  Natural gradient descent      Cramer-Rao bound (geodesic)     |\n|  Sufficient statistics         EM algorithm (e/m projections)  |\n|  Hypothesis testing geometry   Neural network optimization     |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
